212:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="piter">
219:        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><h3>Memorize the roles before the products</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-4-afk-agents/t0521-piter-four-elements.jpg" alt="Four colored letter cards spelling P I T E R for Prompt input, Trigger, Environment, and Review" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">8:41</span>PITER compresses the architecture into four roles: Prompt input, Trigger, Environment, Review.</figcaption></figure><div class="piter-map" aria-label="PITER mapped to course tools"><article data-letter="PI"><h4>Prompt input</h4><p>The request, outcome, and constraints.</p><code>GitHub issue</code></article><article data-letter="T"><h4>Trigger</h4><p>The event that starts the run.</p><code>webhook · cron · manual</code></article><article data-letter="E"><h4>Environment</h4><p>The bounded place with tools and credentials.</p><code>dedicated Mac mini / checkout</code></article><article data-letter="R"><h4>Review</h4><p>The evidence and decision surface.</p><code>issue feed · plan · PR</code></article></div></div>
221:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><h3>Demo 1 instantiates all four</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-4-afk-agents/t0605-webhook-environment-ready.png" alt="Screen-shared Mac mini terminal running the ADW webhook trigger beside a GitHub issue form" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">10:05</span>Trigger and environment made concrete: <code>trigger_webhook.py</code> listens on port 8001 in the dedicated Mac mini while GitHub supplies the request surface.</figcaption></figure><p>Prompt input is issue #31. The webhook fires when an issue opens or when an issue comment is exactly <code>adw</code>. To answer GitHub quickly, the webhook launches the workflow in a background process rather than holding the request open. The environment is Dan’s dedicated Mac mini, reached through a tunnel. Review is PR #32 plus the issue-comment live feed. Demo 2 later swaps the trigger to a manual command and the environment to the local device while leaving prompt input and review intact.</p></div>
231:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>Today’s hosted systems are still PITER</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>GitHub-native and vendor-hosted coding agents often bundle the trigger, sandboxed environment, and pull-request review surface. PITER remains useful precisely because it lets you audit the bundle. Ask what event launches work, what the sandbox can reach, and what evidence appears before merge.</p><p class="current-practice__source">Source: TAC-4 current-practice extract; re-check at publication.</p></aside></div>
233:        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><h3>Rebuild the acronym</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Expand PITER and give the course tool for each element.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Prompt input—GitHub issue; Trigger—webhook, cron, or manual command; Environment—dedicated Mac mini or bounded checkout; Review—issue feed, plan, and pull request.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Which two elements change between Dan’s first and second demos?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Trigger and environment.</p></details></div></section></div>
235:        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><h3>Teach roles without brand names</h3><div class="teach-back"><p>Explain PITER to someone using no product names. Then map it once to the course and once to your own stack.</p></div></div>
238:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="adw">
247:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><h3>One issue number enters a 536-line workflow</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-4-afk-agents/t1630-adw-plan-build-workflow.png" alt="Top of adw_plan_build.py showing the five-step workflow in its docstring" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">27:10</span>The source code itself is the clearest anatomy slide. Note that its branch example has drifted from the actual branch-name template—documentation is reviewable output too.</figcaption></figure><p><code>adws/adw_plan_build.py</code> accepts an issue number and optionally an existing ADW ID. It creates a logger, posts a start message, fetches issue details, asks a classifier which planning template applies, asks another agent to create a branch, runs the planner, finds the created plan, commits it, runs the implementer with the plan path, commits the implementation, and creates the pull request. After each meaningful step, deterministic code checks the response and reports progress to the issue.</p><p>The script is a uv single-file program. Dan’s point is not Python. It could be shell, Bun, or another host language. The point is that ordinary code now contains calls to non-deterministic judgment as normal workflow steps.</p></div>
257:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>The ADW concept outlives the wrapper</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>The course uses Claude Code programmable mode as the agent wrapper. Newer headless and SDK surfaces can provide structured events, sessions, and programmatic controls directly. Treat <code>agent.py</code> as a period implementation. Preserve the ADW’s step boundaries, contracts, artifacts, and reporting when the wrapper changes.</p><p class="current-practice__source">Source: TAC-4 current-practice extract; re-check at publication.</p></aside></div>
259:        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><h3>Recite the unit and sequence</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Define an ADW.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A reusable agentic workflow combining code, agentic prompts, and agents to deliver results autonomously.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What are the five stages of <code>adw_plan_build.py</code>?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Fetch issue, create branch, plan and commit, implement and commit, create pull request.</p></details></div></section></div>
261:        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><h3>Explain why this is not “a script that calls an API”</h3><div class="teach-back"><p>Use the five stages to explain where deterministic code, prompts, and agents each contribute. Name one failure a mega-prompt would hide.</p></div></div>
264:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="agentic-layer">
273:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><h3>The repository tree shows the boundary</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-4-afk-agents/t1460-health-check-healthy.png" alt="VS Code showing the TAC-4 repository tree and a healthy ADW system health check" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">24:20</span>The left rail makes the layer visible: <code>adws/</code>, <code>.claude/</code>, <code>specs/</code>, <code>ai_docs/</code>, <code>scripts/</code>, and logs surround <code>app/</code>.</figcaption></figure><p>Dan calls out that <code>app/</code> is untouched by the infrastructure around it. The two essential directories are <code>.claude/commands/</code>, where reusable prompt templates live, and <code>adws/</code>, where compositional workflows live. Supporting directories supply concrete assets: <code>specs/</code> holds plan artifacts, <code>ai_docs/</code> holds documentation agents can read, <code>scripts/</code> supplies operational commands, and runtime directories hold run evidence.</p></div>
283:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>Repository-native instructions make the layer portable</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Skills and repository-native agent instructions can package reusable behavior more directly than course-era slash commands. The architectural rule stays the same: operational knowledge belongs in the agentic layer, close enough to version with the codebase but separate from application runtime dependencies.</p><p class="current-practice__source">Source: TAC-4 current-practice extract; re-check at publication.</p></aside></div>
285:        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><h3>Name the boundary</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Which two directories form the essential agentic layer in this lesson?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>.claude/commands/</code> for prompt templates and <code>adws/</code> for workflows.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What is the rule about <code>app/</code>?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The layer may operate on the app, but the app remains untouched by and independent from the agentic layer.</p></details></div></section></div>
287:        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><h3>Explain “around, not inside”</h3><div class="teach-back"><p>Point at three directories in the course tree and explain how each supports out-loop work without becoming product runtime code.</p></div></div>
290:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="micro-agents">
299:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><h3>The plan frees the implementer’s context</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-4-afk-agents/t2020-agents-observability-dir.png" alt="VS Code explorer showing nine per-agent folders for ADW run cb881e79" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">33:40</span>The <code>agents/cb881e79/</code> tree makes decomposition inspectable: classifier, branch generator, planner, plan finder, two committers, implementer, PR creator, and workflow log.</figcaption></figure><p>The classifier receives issue data and returns one of three commands. The branch generator receives only what it needs to create a compliant branch. The planner receives the issue and the selected class template. The plan finder locates the new specification. A committer creates a message and commit. Only then does the implementer begin, with a fresh context window and the committed plan as its prompt. It does not need the planner’s internal conversation. It needs the concrete asset the planner produced.</p><p>That boundary is the key. An artifact crosses the step boundary; hidden reasoning does not. The system can inspect, diff, commit, and improve the artifact.</p></div>
309:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>Structured calls can replace whole agents for glue</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Classification, branch naming, and similar glue steps may now be cheaper as structured model calls rather than full coding-agent sessions. Preserve the decomposition: one decision, one schema, one validator. Reserve agentic tool use for steps that genuinely need repository exploration and action.</p><p class="current-practice__source">Source: TAC-4 current-practice extract; re-check at publication.</p></aside></div>
311:        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><h3>Recover the sequence and policy</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">List the eight micro-agent invocations in order.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Issue classifier → branch generator → planner → plan finder → planner committer → implementer → implementer committer → PR creator.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Where should the strongest models go?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Planning and implementation—the steps with the deepest repository reasoning and largest consequences.</p></details></div></section></div>
313:        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><h3>Defend the artifact boundary</h3><div class="teach-back"><p>Explain why the implementer receives a plan file instead of inheriting the planner’s context. Include one benefit for testing, one for observability, and one for model selection.</p></div></div>
316:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="programmable">
329:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><h3><code>execute_template</code> packages one micro-agent run</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-4-afk-agents/t1930-agent-py-programmable-mode.png" alt="agent.py building a Claude Code programmable-mode command with stream-json and dangerously-skip-permissions" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">32:10</span><code>agent.py</code> builds the command, writes the stream to disk, and parses the final result. The dangerous flag is visible because the environment must justify it.</figcaption></figure><p>The wrapper composes a prompt from a slash command and its arguments. The referenced markdown template supplies the actual instructions through the CLI’s command mechanism. Before launch, the wrapper saves the exact prompt under <code>agents/{adw_id}/{agent}/prompts/</code>. It streams JSONL output to disk, produces a readable JSON representation, and parses the final result message for success, session ID, text, and run metadata. A minimal environment allowlist passes the API key, configured Claude path, ordinary process paths, and an optional GitHub token mapping.</p><p>The result is a function-like seam with durable evidence on both sides: exact request and exact event stream. The ADW can call it eight times without pretending the calls are deterministic.</p></div>
339:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>The wrapper is period detail; the callable seam is durable</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Dedicated agent SDK and headless interfaces can expose structured execution more directly than a subprocess wrapper. Migrate when useful, but keep prompt capture, streamed telemetry, final-result parsing, minimal environment exposure, and explicit permission boundaries.</p><p class="current-practice__source">Source: TAC-4 current-practice extract; re-check at publication.</p></aside></div>
341:        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><h3>Write the command from memory</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What is the command shape used by the lesson?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>claude -p "&lt;command and args&gt;" --model &lt;model&gt; --output-format stream-json --verbose --dangerously-skip-permissions</code>.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why is the dangerous flag considered acceptable here?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Only because the workflow is confined to a dedicated, disposable environment with bounded credentials. The environment is the permission boundary.</p></details></div></section></div>
343:        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><h3>Explain “agent as component” precisely</h3><div class="teach-back"><p>Trace one request from Python object to saved prompt, subprocess, JSONL stream, parsed result, and deterministic validator. Do not use the phrase “it just runs Claude.”</p></div></div>
346:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="contracts">
355:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><h3>The feature run crosses five strict seams</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-4-afk-agents/t1540-manual-trigger-kickoff.png" alt="Terminal showing a failed wrong-path launch followed by a successful ADW run with issue class, ADW ID, and branch" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">25:40</span>The successful run emits machine-shaped outputs: ADW ID <code>cb881e79</code>, issue command <code>/feature</code>, branch <code>feat-33-cb881e79-jsonl-support</code>. Above it, Dan’s wrong-directory <code>Failed to spawn</code> remains visible.</figcaption></figure><p>The issue classifier can think freely but must return only <code>/chore</code>, <code>/bug</code>, <code>/feature</code>, or <code>0</code>. The branch generator must return only the branch name. The plan finder must return only a path or <code>0</code>. The commit agent must return only the commit message it used. The PR creator must return only the URL. Each response becomes input to ordinary Python that checks shape, membership, or error status.</p><p>The wrong-path error in the frame teaches the same design ethic at another boundary: a command failed loudly before any work was performed. The repair is not to reinterpret the error. It is to use the actual course path, <code>uv run adws/adw_plan_build.py &lt;issue&gt;</code>.</p></div>
365:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>Schemas strengthen the same idea</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Modern structured-output features can replace some “Return ONLY” text contracts with schemas and typed results. That reduces parsing ambiguity but does not remove validation. The workflow must still reject illegal classes, missing artifacts, and failed actions, then report the failure to the review surface.</p><p class="current-practice__source">Source: TAC-4 current-practice extract; re-check at publication.</p></aside></div>
367:        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><h3>Recover the classifier boundary</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What are the classifier’s only legal outputs?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>/chore</code>, <code>/bug</code>, <code>/feature</code>, or <code>0</code>.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What happens when a step’s contract breaks?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The workflow logs the problem, posts a ❌ status to the issue, and exits rather than guessing.</p></details></div></section></div>
369:        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><h3>Explain why strictness enables autonomy</h3><div class="teach-back"><p>Explain why an agent gets broad freedom inside a task but a narrow contract at the workflow boundary. Use classifier and plan-finder examples.</p></div></div>
372:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="observability">
379:        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><h3>Four views of the same run</h3><div class="telemetry-stack" aria-label="Four observability layers"><article><h4>1 · Live feed</h4><p><code>{adw_id}_{agent}: ✅/❌</code> issue comments show progress where the request lives.</p></article><article><h4>2 · Agent artifacts</h4><p><code>agents/{adw_id}/</code> holds exact prompts, JSONL, readable output, and execution logs.</p></article><article><h4>3 · Session logs</h4><p><code>logs/{session_id}/</code> receives the five configured hook events.</p></article><article><h4>4 · Concrete plan</h4><p><code>specs/*.md</code> records planner output and becomes the implementer’s input.</p></article></div><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-4-afk-agents/t0700-chore-issue-live-feed.png" alt="GitHub issue #31 showing ADW progress comments with tracking ID 5615f7c8" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">11:40</span>The live feed threads run ID <code>5615f7c8</code> through classification, branch creation, and planning. It is a status surface, not the full debugging record.</figcaption></figure></div>
381:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><h3>The bad PR is the lesson’s best evidence</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-4-afk-agents/t0890-chore-pr-review.png" alt="Pull request #32 showing implementation plan, changes, files, ADW tracking ID, and closing issue reference" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">14:50</span>PR #32 is cleanly structured and factually imperfect. It expands ADW incorrectly and lists plausible files that do not exist.</figcaption></figure><p>The instinct is to edit the PR description and move on. Dan rejects that instinct. A hand edit repairs one artifact. The next documentation chore will use the same prompt and can repeat the same mistake. The durable repair belongs in the owning template: define ADW as “AI Developer Workflow,” require file claims to be checked against the repository, and improve the PR template if its report invites speculation.</p><p>The repository contains a second honest exhibit. Its agent-written <code>adws/README.md</code> points model selection to “agent.py line 129,” although the real choices are represented in type literals and per-step requests. Its commit-format section conflicts with <code>commit.md</code>. Review is not a ceremony after autonomy; it is the input to system improvement.</p></div>
385:        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example</p><h3>Turn one false file claim into a reusable check</h3><ol><li><strong>Observe:</strong> PR #32 describes files absent from the repository.</li><li><strong>Trace:</strong> use ADW ID <code>5615f7c8</code> to find the PR creator’s prompt and output.</li><li><strong>Locate ownership:</strong> decide whether the false claim originated in the implementation report or <code>pull_request.md</code>.</li><li><strong>Encode:</strong> add an instruction to describe only files that exist and verify names against the diff.</li><li><strong>Reset:</strong> clear issue comments and delete the test PR with the course scripts.</li><li><strong>Rerun:</strong> use the same issue so the input stays stable.</li><li><strong>Compare:</strong> diff the two PR bodies. The improvement passes only if the error class disappears without hand editing.</li></ol><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-4-afk-agents/t2245-jsonl-feature-verified.png" alt="Running app showing a successfully uploaded JSONL table with flattened nested columns and list indices" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">37:25</span>Review ends in behavior, not prose. The app shows a five-row table with <code>__</code> and <code>_0</code> column conventions from the original issue.</figcaption></figure></div>
391:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>Add structured telemetry without losing the simple surfaces</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Production systems may add check runs, job summaries, trace-style spans, queues, and cost dashboards. The course’s issue feed remains an excellent zero-infrastructure status surface. Keep the four evidence layers even when richer telemetry arrives: user-visible progress, exact per-agent artifacts, session-level logs, and committed plans.</p><p class="current-practice__source">Source: TAC-4 current-practice extract; re-check at publication.</p></aside></div>
393:        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><h3>Name the evidence before the doctrine</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What are the four observability layers?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Issue-comment live feed; <code>agents/{adw_id}/</code> prompts and outputs; <code>logs/{session_id}/</code> hook logs; committed plan file.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What does “fix the system, not the issue” require?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Trace a bad output to its owning template, contract, workflow step, or environment condition; improve that source; rerun and compare instead of hand-patching the artifact.</p></details></div></section></div>
395:        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><h3>Tell the story of one compounding correction</h3><div class="teach-back"><p>Use PR #32 to explain symptom → trace → owning template → rerun → comparison. Your listener should be able to name the one-off fix you deliberately refused.</p></div></div>
398:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="adoption">
407:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><h3>Dan’s setup catches the viewer’s most likely mistake</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-4-afk-agents/t1460-health-check-healthy.png" alt="Terminal output showing Overall Status HEALTHY, repository warning, GitHub CLI checks, and Claude Code test" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">24:20</span>The course health check validates environment variables, Git remote, GitHub CLI, and a live Claude Code round-trip, then can post the result to the issue.</figcaption></figure><p>The install command removes the course remote, initializes the working repository, copies environment samples, installs dependencies, and reports the actions it cannot complete for you. You must create and push to a repository you own because the ADW needs to create your issues, branches, comments, and pull requests. The health check then warns if the remote still points at <code>disler</code>, verifies GitHub CLI installation and authentication, checks required paths and environment values, and performs a live 2+2 Claude round-trip expecting <code>4</code>.</p><p>Only after that does the course run the two-line documentation chore. The chore is not a toy to skip. It is the cheap rehearsal that proves every PITER element and every ADW stage before the feature spends eight agent invocations.</p></div>
417:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>Serialize the course workflow or isolate every run</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>The course ADW uses one working checkout and changes branches in place. Concurrent webhook launches can collide. For this lab, serialize triggers. Production systems commonly add a queue or lock and use a worktree or container per run. Parallelism belongs after one serial path is trustworthy.</p><p class="current-practice__source">Source: TAC-4 current-practice extract; re-check at publication.</p></aside></div>
419:        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><h3>State the gate and ladder</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What does the course health check verify?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Environment variables and Claude path, Git remote, GitHub CLI installation/authentication, a live Claude Code round-trip, and optionally the issue-comment path.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What is the adoption ladder?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Chores → bugs → features.</p></details></div></section></div>
421:        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><h3>Explain why pre-flight belongs inside the layer</h3><div class="teach-back"><p>Teach why a health check is not merely setup documentation. Your answer must connect repeatability, error attribution, issue-visible evidence, and the trust ladder.</p></div></div>
424:      <section class="chapter-section" id="current-practice">
447:      <section class="chapter-section" id="lab">
454:        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-setup">
455:          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 4.0 · Build your PITER perimeter</h3>
456:          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>25 min</li><li>Gate</li><li>Course repo only</li></ul>
457:          <ol class="lab-box__steps"><li>Clone <code>https://github.com/disler/tac-4.git</code> into a fresh directory and enter it.</li><li>Read <code>.claude/commands/install.md</code> before running <code>/install</code>. Say what it will do: remove the course remote, initialize Git, copy environment samples, install frontend and backend dependencies, and attempt the TAC-2 environment copy.</li><li>Create a new empty GitHub repository you own. Add it as <code>origin</code> and push <code>main</code>.</li><li>Fill the root <code>.env</code> with <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code> and <code>CLAUDE_CODE_PATH</code>. Use <code>which claude</code> when the subprocess cannot find the CLI. Fill <code>app/server/.env</code> for the application provider.</li><li>Audit <code>.claude/settings.json</code>. Record the interactive Bash allowlist, the three denied command patterns (two force-push forms and <code>rm -rf</code>), the five hook events, and the <code>logs/{session_id}/</code> destination.</li><li>Explain why the ADW run does not rely on those interactive permissions: <code>execute_template</code> enables dangerous-skip, so environment isolation is the boundary.</li><li>Create a warm-up issue in your repository and run <code>uv run adws/health_check.py &lt;number&gt;</code>.</li></ol>
461:        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-chore">
462:          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 4.1 · First out-loop chore</h3>
463:          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>25 min</li><li>Zero mid-run intervention</li><li>Evidence audit</li></ul>
464:          <ol class="lab-box__steps"><li>Create issue A titled <code>ADW Documentation</code>. Use the course shape: <code>/chore</code> followed by a request to read the ADW directory and update its README with how it works.</li><li>Launch <code>uv run adws/adw_plan_build.py &lt;A&gt;</code>. If you run <code>uv run adw_plan_build.py</code> from the root and see <code>Failed to spawn</code>, correct the path rather than debugging the workflow.</li><li>Go hands off. Narrate the issue feed: start → classification → branch → planner → plan → first commit → implementer → second commit → PR.</li><li>Verify a branch matching <code>&lt;class&gt;-&lt;issue&gt;-&lt;adw-id&gt;-&lt;slug&gt;</code>, a new <code>specs/*-plan.md</code>, two commits matching <code>&lt;agent&gt;: &lt;class&gt;: &lt;message&gt;</code>, and a PR containing plan link, changes checklist, <code>Closes #A</code>, and the tracking ID.</li><li>Audit every PR claim against repository reality. For each inaccuracy, name the owning template or generated artifact. If there are none, write “none found” and state what you checked.</li></ol>
468:        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-feature">
469:          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 4.2 · Reproduce the JSONL feature run</h3>
470:          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>35 min active review</li><li>15–20 min run observed in course</li><li>pytest + live app</li></ul>
471:          <ol class="lab-box__steps"><li>Start the app with <code>sh scripts/start.sh</code>. At <code>localhost:5173</code>, confirm that the shipped app accepts only CSV and JSON. The server rejects other extensions; JSONL support does not exist on main.</li><li>Create issue B titled <code>Jsonl Support</code>. Require a full-file field scan, standard library only, one table per file, nested keys and lists joined with centrally configurable <code>__</code>, list indexes such as <code>_0</code>, interface copy, and multiple test fixtures.</li><li>Run the health check on issue B and require <code>HEALTHY</code>.</li><li>Launch <code>uv run adws/adw_plan_build.py &lt;B&gt;</code>.</li><li>In a second terminal, follow <code>agents/&lt;id&gt;/adw_plan_build/execution.log</code>. After each agent completes, inspect its saved prompt and result. Record <code>num_turns</code>, <code>duration_ms</code>, and <code>total_cost_usd</code>.</li><li>Review the committed plan before the diff. Then check out the feature branch, run <code>cd app/server &amp;&amp; uv run pytest</code>, start the app, regression-test an old query, upload an agent-created JSONL fixture, and confirm the table uses <code>__</code> and <code>_0</code>.</li></ol>
475:        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-triggers">
476:          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 4.3 · Swap the trigger</h3>
477:          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>20 min</li><li>Manual + cron + webhook attempt</li></ul>
478:          <ol class="lab-box__steps"><li>Run <code>uv run adws/trigger_cron.py</code>. Comment exactly <code>adw</code> on issue A and observe reprocessing within roughly twenty seconds. State the two qualifying conditions: a new issue with no comments, or a latest comment exactly equal to <code>adw</code>.</li><li>Attempt the webhook: run <code>uv run adws/trigger_webhook.py</code> on port 8001; expose it with <code>scripts/expose_webhook.sh</code> (requires <code>CLOUDFLARED_TUNNEL_TOKEN</code>) or ngrok; configure GitHub Issues and Issue-comment events; create a tiny chore from your phone. If infrastructure blocks you, document the attempted configuration and exact failure.</li><li>Read <code>GET /health</code> and connect its summary to the standalone health check.</li><li>Stop the trigger and apps with <code>scripts/kill_trigger_webhook.sh</code> and <code>scripts/stop_apps.sh</code>. Explain why a forgotten listener is a cost bug.</li><li>For reruns, use <code>scripts/clear_issue_comments.sh &lt;issue&gt;</code> and <code>scripts/delete_pr.sh &lt;pr&gt; [--delete-branch]</code>.</li></ol>
482:        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-stretch">
483:          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 4.4 · System-improvement stretches</h3>
484:          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>Choose 2</li><li>30–45 min</li><li>Still zero hand-typed app code</li></ul>
485:          <ol class="lab-box__steps"><li><strong>New problem class:</strong> through Claude Code, add <code>/docs</code> or <code>/test</code> as a new template, extend the command Literals in <code>adws/data_types.py</code>, and update classification. Prove an issue runs end to end.</li><li><strong>Fix the system:</strong> encode the worst checkpoint-1 error into the owning template. Reset and rerun the same issue. Diff the PRs to demonstrate a class-level correction.</li><li><strong>Break pre-flight:</strong> temporarily remove the API key or point the remote back at the course. Compare the standalone health check, webhook health endpoint, and real ADW failure. Restore the environment.</li><li><strong>Model policy:</strong> through Claude Code, switch planner and implementer requests to the stronger model option. Rerun a feature-class issue and compare plan depth, turns, duration, and cost with the saved baseline.</li><li><strong>KPI scorecard:</strong> score presence, size, attempts, and streak for the chore and feature. Name the system change that would improve the weakest number.</li><li><strong>Course transfer map:</strong> map all four PITER elements in <code>tac/tac-4</code>, then list three additional course-repo chores the existing templates could accept. Circle the safest next issue, but keep every action inside this course repository.</li></ol>
498:        <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>All pass criteria</h4><ul><li>Zero application code typed by hand.</li><li>Healthy check against your repo, no course-remote warning, ✅ issue comment.</li><li>Chore completed with live feed, compliant branch, committed plan, two compliant commits, and complete PR.</li><li>Written factual audit mapping each issue to an owning template or artifact.</li><li>JSONL feature completed with green tests and live <code>__</code>/<code>_0</code> verification.</li><li>ADW ID traced across comments, branch, agent artifacts, commits, and PR.</li><li>Cron demonstrated; webhook demonstrated or attempted with evidence.</li><li>All listeners and apps stopped.</li><li>Oral close: PITER, ADW definition, classifier contract, safety boundary, and fix-the-system policy without notes.</li></ul></div>
501:      <section class="chapter-section" id="mastery">
507:        <section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Expand PITER and map each element to the first demo.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Prompt input—GitHub issue #31; Trigger—GitHub webhook; Environment—dedicated Mac mini; Review—issue feed, committed artifacts, and PR #32.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What moves out of the loop, and what human work remains?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The execution transitions move out: classify, branch, plan, implement, commit, and create PR. The human still supplies the request and constraints, reviews evidence, and improves the system.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why can the manual and webhook demos use the same ADW?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The trigger and environment are swappable PITER roles; the workflow, prompt-input shape, and review surface remain stable.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Which work remains in-loop?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Experimentation, new territory, and very hard specific problems.</p></details></div></section>
510:        <section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">5. Define an ADW and list its five stages.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Reusable workflow combining code, prompts, and agents to deliver results autonomously; fetch issue → branch → plan and commit → implement and commit → pull request.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">6. List the eight feature-run agent invocations.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Classifier, branch generator, planner, plan finder, planner committer, implementer, implementer committer, PR creator.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">7. Why is the committed plan a better boundary than planner conversation history?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It is concrete, reviewable, diffable, committable, improvable, and lets the implementer start with a fresh context window.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">8. What is the classifier contract and failure policy?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Only <code>/chore</code>, <code>/bug</code>, <code>/feature</code>, or <code>0</code>; illegal output is logged, reported with ❌, and stops the run.</p></details></div></section>
513:        <section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">9. Why is dangerous-skip safe here, and what would make it unsafe?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It is acceptable only in a dedicated, disposable environment with bounded credentials. A daily-driver checkout, production secrets, or broad filesystem access breaks the argument.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">10. Name the four observability layers.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Issue live feed; per-agent prompts and outputs; hook session logs; committed plan.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">11. What does the health check prove—and not prove?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It proves the environment, remote, GitHub CLI, Claude round-trip, and comment path are ready. It does not prove a particular plan or implementation will be correct.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">12. Why must the lab serialize runs?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The course workflow changes branches in one checkout. Concurrent triggers can collide without a queue, lock, worktree, or container per run.</p></details></div></section>
516:        <section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">13. A PR uses the wrong expansion of ADW. What do you do?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Trace the claim to the owning prompt or report, encode the canonical definition, rerun the same issue, and compare. Do not merely edit the PR.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">14. Why does “own the pipeline” survive using a hosted agent?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Hosting can be rented while repository-specific templates, workflow semantics, contracts, standards, and review policy remain yours.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">15. Which model policy is durable after course-era names change?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Use the cheapest model that reliably clears each step; reserve the strongest models for planning and implementation.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">16. What is the adoption ladder, and why?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Chores → bugs → features. It builds trust and improves templates with low-cost, reviewable work before larger autonomous changes.</p></details></div></section>
520:        <h3>Capstone teach-back</h3>
521:        <div class="teach-back"><p>Without notes, teach the complete system in seven minutes:</p><ol><li>Route one task in-loop and one out-loop.</li><li>Map PITER for issue #31.</li><li>Define the ADW and walk its five stages.</li><li>Explain micro-agent boundaries and model policy.</li><li>Write the programmable-mode command shape.</li><li>State the classifier contract and loud-failure rule.</li><li>Use PR #32 to teach “fix the system, not the issue.”</li></ol><p class="fine">Expert pass: your listener can predict why two simultaneous webhook events are unsafe in the course checkout and propose the missing isolation without changing the meaning of PITER.</p></div>
